<p>
Today while I was going through my feed, I saw this <a href="https://bicycleforyourmind.com/expanding-with-typinator10">this post</a> from macosxguru over at <a href="https://bicycleforyourmind.com">Bicycle For Your Mind</a>. It&rsquo;s about his system for using snippets on his system. The TL;DR is that he has settled on <a href="https://ergonis.com/en/typinator/">Typinator</a> and likes it a lot.
</p>
<p>
I use snippets a lot but use several systems—<a href="https://github.com/joaotavora/yasnippet">YASnippet</a>, abbrev mode, and the macOS text expansion facility—but none of them work everywhere I need them to so I have to negotiate three different systems. YASnippet is different from the other two in that its snippets can accept input instead of just making a text substation like the others.
</p>
<p>
In his post, macosxguru mentions that his previous system for text substitutions was based on the <a href="https://www.alfredapp.com/">Alfred</a> snippet functions. I&rsquo;ve been using Alfred for a long time and love it. A one time purchase of the power pack makes your Mac <i>much</i> more powerful. Still, even though I was vaguely aware of it, I&rsquo;d never used Alfred&rsquo;s snippet function.
</p>
<p>
After seeing it mentioned on macosxguru&rsquo;s post I decided to try it out. It&rsquo;s easy to specify text substitutions. I couldn&rsquo;t immediately figure out how to trigger the substitutions manually so I just set them to trigger automatically. I usually don&rsquo;t like that but so far it&rsquo;s working out well.
</p>
<p>
Up til now, I haven&rsquo;t found anywhere that the substitutions don&rsquo;t work. That can&rsquo;t be said of any of the other systems I was using. It&rsquo;s particularly hard to find one that works with both Emacs <i>and</i> other macOS applications.
</p>
<p>
If you&rsquo;re using Emacs on macOS, you should definitely look into Alfred. It plays very nicely with Emacs and my newfound snippets ability makes the combination even better.</p>
